.TH AS86 1
.SH NAME
as86 \- assembler for the 8086
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B as86
[\ \-0123agjOuwk\ ] [\ \-o\ objfile\ ] [\ \-l\ [listfile]\ ] [\ \-m\ [listfile]\ ]
[\ \-n\ name\ ] [\ \-b\ binfile\ ] [\ \-s\ symfile\ ] [\ \-t\ n\ ] file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B as86
assembles the assembly language program in
.I file
(\- for the standard input) into an object file for
.BR ld86 (1).
.BR cc (1)
runs it on the compiler's output and on
.B .s
files; it can also be run alone.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 14
.BI \-o " objfile"
Write the object file to
.IR objfile .
Without
.B \-o
(or
.BR \-b ),
as86 only checks the program.
.TP
.BR \-0 ", " \-1 ", " \-2
Assemble 16-bit code, for the 8086, the 80186 or the 80286. cc uses
.BR \-0 .
.TP
.B \-3
Assemble 32-bit code for the 80386. A program for XENIX on the 8086
cannot use it.
.TP
.B \-a
Another assembler's syntax: round and square brackets exchange their
meanings, and jumps and calls through a register are written
differently.
.TP
.B \-g
Put only the global symbols in the object file.
.TP
.B \-j
Make every jump a long one; a conditional branch becomes a short
branch around a long jump.
.TP
.B \-O
Optimize: make more passes over the program, so that each jump to a
label further on can be as short as its distance allows. The passes go
on until no label moves.
.B "cc \-O"
uses it.
.TP
.B \-u
Take undefined symbols to be imported, from a segment not given,
instead of reporting them.
.TP
.B \-w
Do not print warnings.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the object file even when there are errors.
.TP
.BR \-l " [\fIlistfile\fP]"
Write a listing: to
.IR listfile ,
or to the standard output.
.TP
.BR \-m " [\fIlistfile\fP]"
A listing with the expansions of macros.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Give the module the name
.I name
in the object file, instead of the source file's name.
.TP
.BI \-b " binfile"
Write a raw binary image of the program, with no header, to
.IR binfile .
.TP
.BI \-s " symfile"
Write the symbols, as text, to
.IR symfile .
.TP
.BI \-t " n"
Put the text in segment
.IR n +3.
.SS Syntax
A line holds a label, an instruction or a directive, and its operands,
in the order of Intel's assembler:
.PP
.nf
	_square:
		push	bp
		mov	bp,sp
		mov	ax,4[bp]	! the first argument
		imul	ax
		pop	bp
		ret
.fi
.PP
A label ends with a colon. ! and ; begin comments.
Numbers are decimal; $ or 0x before one makes it hexadecimal, % binary.
A
.B #
marks an immediate operand:
.B "mov ax,#10"
puts 10 in ax, while
.B "mov ax,10"
loads the word at address 10. Square brackets mark an indirect
operand:
.BR "mov ax,[bx]" ;
an offset may come before them, as in
.BR "mov ax,_table[bx]" .
.PP
Besides the instructions of the 8086, with the 8087's, there are:
.TP 14
.B br
the longest unconditional jump
.TP
.B "beq, bne, ..."
conditional branches that reach any distance, as a short branch around
a long jump: beq bne blt ble bgt bge blo blos bhi bhis bcc bcs bmi bpl
bvc bvs
.TP
.B "callf, jmpf"
far calls and jumps
.TP
.B "movb, cmpb, ..."
byte forms of the instructions, where the operands do not show the size
.SS Directives
.TP 22
.B ".text, .data, .bss"
choose the segment to assemble into
.TP
.B "export, .globl"
make labels global: export only those defined here; .globl also
imports those that are not
.TP
.B "import, extern"
use labels defined in another file
.TP
.B ".byte, db"
bytes
.TP
.B ".word, dw"
16-bit words
.TP
.B ".long, dd"
32-bit long words
.TP
.B ".ascii"
a string;
.B .asciz
a string and a NUL
.TP
.B ".blkb, .space"
reserve bytes;
.B .blkw
words
.TP
.B ".comm, .lcomm"
a common area, global or local
.TP
.B ".even, .align"
align the location
.TP
.B "equ, set"
give a label a value;
.B set
may give it another later
.TP
.B "if, else, endif"
assemble lines only if a value is not 0
.TP
.B "include, get"
read another source file here
.TP
.B "macro, mend"
define a macro; ?1, ?2 ... in its body stand for its arguments
.SH FILES
.TP 14
a.out
.PD 0
.TP
file.o
the object file
.PD
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors and warnings are printed with the line of the source and a
description. The exit status is 0 if there were no errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
cc(1), ld86(1), nm(1), adb(1)
.PP
Intel Corporation,
.IR "iAPX 86/88, 186/188 User's Manual" .
.SH HISTORY
An
.B as
command first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.
.SH AUTHORS
as86 was written by Bruce Evans.
.SH BUGS
A source line may be at most 256 characters long.
.PP
as86 does not check that an instruction is one the processor named by
.BR \-0 ,
.B \-1
or
.B \-2
has: an instruction of the 80186 or a later processor assembles without
a warning, and the 8086 cannot run it.
